1. Pre-paying for the growth
   String::with_capacity(100)  len   0  capacity 100
   after 10 pushes             len  10  capacity 100
   assert_eq!(capacity, 100) held — the buffer was bought once, up front.
   (An empty String::new() would have reallocated on the way to 10.)

2. Digits out of a raw field
   read_score(" 42 \n" ) -> Ok(42)
   read_score("42"     ) -> Ok(42)
   read_score("4 2"    ) -> Err(invalid digit found in string)
   read_score(""       ) -> Err(cannot parse integer from empty string)
   The trim is doing real work — `parse` itself refuses the spaces:
   " 42 \n".parse::<i32>()        -> Err(ParseIntError { kind: InvalidDigit })
   The Result is the whole point — a bad field is data, not a crash.

3. One String out of three types
   "seat 3: 61.84% turnout, decided = true"
   {:.2} rounds for display only; the f64 itself is untouched.

4. Bytes that really are UTF-8
   String::from_utf8(7 bytes) -> Ok("★ Ada")
   No copy: from_utf8 takes the Vec<u8> and hands back the same allocation.

5. Bytes that are not
   from_utf8       -> Err: invalid utf-8 sequence of 1 bytes from index 2
                      first bad byte at index 2
                      and the Vec is handed back unharmed: 5 bytes
   from_utf8_lossy -> "Ad�a�"   <- each bad byte became U+FFFD
   5 bytes in, 5 chars out, 2 of them the replacement character.
   Lossy never fails, which is the risk: it silences the error rather than
   reporting it. Use it for display; use from_utf8 when the bytes mattered.
